\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{representation-theory}

\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The positive CAR product has half-staircase weight}

\p{Let #{K} be a field in which #{2} is invertible. Begin with #{N=2}
and put #{v_+=d_{01}}. The relations are

##{d_{01}^2=d_{10}^2=0,\qquad d_{00}^2=d_{11}^2=1,
\qquad d_{01}d_{10}+d_{10}d_{01}=2.}

The only raising operator is

##{D(E_{01})=\frac12(d_{00}d_{01}+d_{01}d_{11}),}

and each summand kills #{v_+}. For the diagonal operators, the same CAR
relations give

##{D(E_{00})v_+=\frac32v_+,\qquad
D(E_{11})v_+=\frac12v_+.}

Thus #{v_+} has weight #{(3/2,1/2)}, and
#{D(I)v_+=2v_+}. Subtracting the central correction
#{(N/2)\operatorname{Tr}} leaves #{(1/2,-1/2)}. In characteristic
zero this is the usual #{\mathfrak{sl}_2} value #{\rho}.}

\p{For general #{N}, order the indices as
#{0<1<\cdots<N-1} and form the lexicographically ordered product

##{v_+=\prod_{0\leq i<j<N}^{\mathrm{lex}}d_{ij}.}

Every positive matrix unit is #{Q_{\mathrm{tr}}}-null, and distinct
positive matrix units are polar-orthogonal. Their Clifford generators
therefore anticommute. PBW linear independence shows that the ordered
product is nonzero over a field.}

\p{Let #{i<j}. In

##{D(E_{ij})v_+=\frac12\sum_kd_{ik}d_{kj}v_+,}

a term with #{k<j} vanishes because the positive generator #{d_{kj}}
already occurs in #{v_+}. If #{k\geq j}, then #{i<k}; first
anticommute #{d_{ik}} past #{d_{kj}}, using #{i\ne j}, and the repeated
positive factor #{d_{ik}} makes the term zero. Hence every raising
operator annihilates #{v_+}.}

\p{For a diagonal generator,

##{D(E_{ii})v_+=\frac12\sum_kd_{ik}d_{ki}v_+.}

The contributions for #{k<i}, #{k=i}, and #{k>i} are respectively
#{0}, #{v_+}, and #{2v_+}. Therefore

##{D(E_{ii})v_+
=\frac12\bigl(1+2|\{k:k>i\}|\bigr)v_+.}

For #{i\in\operatorname{Fin}(N)}, the weight and its quadratic-lift
part are

##{\lambda_i=N-i-\frac12,\qquad
\lambda_i-\frac N2=\frac{N-1}{2}-i,\qquad
\sum_i\lambda_i=\frac{N^2}{2}.}

The last equality agrees with the direct central calculation
#{D(I)=N^2/2}.}

\p{TauCeti defines this ordered product and proves its nonvanishing,
raising equations, and diagonal weight. See the exact pinned
\href{https://github.com/TauCetiProject/TauCeti/blob/1a573226a3d9f2c460a6cea6ff3cf2dc82b6a2a3/TauCeti/Algebra/Lie/GeneralLinear/CAR/HighestWeight.lean#L142-L244}{product and PBW nonvanishing}
and
\href{https://github.com/TauCetiProject/TauCeti/blob/1a573226a3d9f2c460a6cea6ff3cf2dc82b6a2a3/TauCeti/Algebra/Lie/GeneralLinear/CAR/HighestWeight.lean#L389-L438}{half-staircase theorems}.
Nonvanishing needs a field; the highest-vector statement adds invertible
#{2}. Identifying the subtracted weight with the rational staircase
requires characteristic zero.}

\p{\citet{Section 2, Example 2.5(1), preprint p. 8}{panyushev2001exterior}
identifies, over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero,
the reduced adjoint spin module with highest weight #{\rho}. The
subtracted weight above is exactly that semisimple staircase. The
added #{(N/2)\operatorname{Tr}} character records the reductive center of
this normal-ordered left action. This comparison does not attribute the
selected product or the full left-regular CAR module to Panyushev, and
it makes no irreducibility or whole-module decomposition claim.}
